    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> It's the Rainforest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Productive: Contribute to storytelling sessions with simple sentences based on visual prompts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Interactive: Relay short messages from one person to another in a group setting.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Can contribute simple sentences to a story from a visual prompt (e.g., "The monkey sleeps in the tree.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can relay short messages or instructions about animals and their habitats to classmates in group activities (e.g., "The sloth is slow.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can use basic language to exchange information about nature activities, using gestures if needed (e.g., "The cat climbs the tree.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Rainforest Animal Sounds" activity. The teacher plays audio recordings of rainforest animal sounds (monkey, parrot, frog, jaguar). Students listen and mimic the sounds, then point to corresponding flashcards of the animals. This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and connects to the theme. Teacher asks, "What animal is it?" for each sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a sloth and says slowly, clearly, and with exaggerated movements, "This is a sloth. It is slow. The sloth is slow." (pronunciation /s/ in sloth, adjectives + nouns). The teacher models descriptive sentences using gestures. Then, for a monkey, "This is a monkey. It can climb and swing." The teacher models asking and answering simple WH-questions: "What animal is it?" "It's a monkey."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of target vocabulary and concepts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the sloth fast or slow?" (Expected answer: "Slow!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a parrot flashcard) "Is this a frog or a parrot?" (Expected answer: "A parrot!")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces initial sounds /j/ for jungle and /r/ for rainforest.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a "Rainforest Story Map" using large, colorful picture cards or a projected slideshow depicting a simple narrative of rainforest animals and their actions in their habitat. Each card shows an animal (e.g., monkey, sloth, toucan, frog, jaguar) interacting with its environment (e.g., climbing vines, sleeping in a tree, flying near a waterfall, jumping on a leaf). The teacher uses demonstratives "this" and "that" and present simple tense to describe each scene.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A sequence of 5-7 large, laminated picture cards illustrating simple actions of rainforest animals. Each card includes elements like "rainforest," "jungle," "vines," "branch," "leaf," "river," "waterfall."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to identify and name rainforest animals and describe their actions or traits using simple sentences, enhancing their ability to interact about their environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher shows the story map cards one by one, describing each image. Then, the teacher asks gist questions for students to point and say:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a monkey swinging) "What does the monkey do?" (Expected answer: "Swing!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a sloth sleeping) "Where does the sloth sleep?" (Expected answer: "In the tree!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the jaguar quiet or noisy?" (Expected answer: "Quiet!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing animals using adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful parrot, jumpy frog), asking and answering WH-questions (e.g., "What animal is it?"), and using present simple verbs (e.g., live, grow, climb, swing, fly, sleep) in simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Animal Description Chain (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher starts by showing a picture of a "slow sloth." Teacher says, "This is a slow sloth." The next student repeats, "This is a slow sloth," and adds another animal with an adjective (e.g., "This is a slow sloth and a colorful toucan."). Students take turns adding a new animal with an adjective, repeating the chain. This reinforces adjective + noun pairs and present simple in a fun, repetitive way, focusing on /s/ and /r/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "What am I?" Guessing Game (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 rainforest animal flashcards (e.g., monkey, parrot, frog, jaguar). One student secretly picks a card and describes the animal using 1-2 simple sentences and gestures, e.g., "It is noisy. It can fly." or "It is slow. It sleeps in the tree." The other student guesses, "Is it a parrot?" or "Is it a sloth?". They swap roles. Teacher circulates, providing support and correction, emphasizing correct pronunciation and vocabulary use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Sentence Builders (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students are given sentence starters and word banks on small whiteboards or paper. For example: "The ___ is ___." (Word bank: monkey, sloth, toucan, colorful, slow, furry, climb, swing, fly). In pairs, students create simple sentences about rainforest animals. E.g., Student A says, "The monkey can climb." Student B writes, "The monkey can climb." They check each other's spoken and written sentences for accuracy. This activity helps students formulate clear descriptions and exchange information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Rainforest Story Relay"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (4-5 students). Each group receives a set of large sequential picture cards depicting a simple story about rainforest animals (e.g., a monkey swinging, a frog jumping, a jaguar hiding, a parrot flying). The first student in each group looks at the first card and says one simple sentence about it (e.g., "The monkey swings on the vines."). They then whisper this sentence to the next student, who adds another sentence based on the next card (e.g., "The frog jumps on a leaf."), whispering it to the next. The last student in the group says the complete story aloud to the teacher or class, combining all the sentences. Gestures are encouraged for clarity.</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task format:</b> Group storytelling and message relay, fostering interactive speaking.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> "It's the Rainforest" and "Discovering Nature’s Wonders" by creating a collaborative narrative about rainforest life.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in relaying simple messages, collaborative interaction, and contributing simple sentences using target vocabulary (rainforest, monkey, frog, jaguar, parrot, vines, leaf) and grammar (Present simple, adjectives + nouns).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Rainforest Story Relay" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Rainforest Story Relay" by:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contributing one simple, descriptive sentence based on a visual prompt (e.g., "The monkey climbs the tree.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relaying a short message accurately to their group members.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist - Yes/No for each student):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can contribute a simple sentence about an animal/action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., sloth, monkey, climb, fly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful parrot).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is mostly intelligible (e.g., /s/ in sloth, /r/ in rainforest).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively participates in relaying the message.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing and sentence on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw your favorite rainforest animal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Complete the sentence: "I like the _____. It is _____. "</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Learner and Teacher Reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new animal did you talk about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to tell your story? What was fun?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific speaking activities worked best to encourage active participation and accurate sentence formation? The "Rainforest Story Relay" showed good engagement and message relay. The "What Am I?" game was effective for practicing questions and descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with forming simple sentences or relaying messages? Some students needed more explicit modeling and sentence frames (e.g., "The _____ can _____.") during the relay. More choral repetition of adjective + noun pairs would be beneficial.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally describe rainforest animals and their actions. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by guiding students to write simple sentences and short descriptive paragraphs about rainforest animals, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>